#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b613bf7-a641-547f-8487-d6af0da0f724" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Equivalent Definition of the Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d5ff7cc-8b3f-5a39-aa5b-ab6f585428d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506701} {:answer/id #uuid "11e63def-7844-5262-ab80-238e82afc4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506715} {:answer/id #uuid "3f7e125d-4a64-5403-b4f5-240ac6952b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506826} {:answer/id #uuid "7c767c64-2e2b-560b-95b1-27629324438c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506952} {:answer/id #uuid "ff94670c-c1ce-57cc-9599-b00476cfd5ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d5ff7cc-8b3f-5a39-aa5b-ab6f585428d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506701} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b3df459-f0ab-5ba2-a113-901da7e7d2cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac305827-2b68-5246-9f72-b59f76116099" :question/math-academy-id "q-104948" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{f(x)-f(2)}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{2}^{2}}{x-2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{x-2}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(2).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{f}^{′}(2)=2\\cdot 2=4,\n$$\n\nand therefore we conclude that $L=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20df0d0d-a94e-55e6-8d19-25a566ab794d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits Equivalent to a Derivative Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb6a2c0-9b12-5d02-8114-ec93e9fd1dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506895} {:answer/id #uuid "96e83254-6e53-5553-a48a-26aa54776b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507006} {:answer/id #uuid "a27838c1-ffc6-51e6-ad62-1715c0672f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507029} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ec119b-815d-53e3-b075-5f37bb3efbc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507069} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fd154a-9418-5779-9f22-3520453bf8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb6a2c0-9b12-5d02-8114-ec93e9fd1dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506895} :answer-field/id #uuid "008a3a74-eacb-51da-8605-e2fef59db043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507240}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbf6525f-a6b2-5d79-bc38-725ad8dae9c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-104976" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e03f453156d57af94b13b76e22dc366644b18f56acc5f9a5f4a2c005e6bec3f7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\nI. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h}=-1$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h}=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Comparing these limits with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwe see that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(0), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(1), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function consists of three line segments. Inspecting the graph, we see that\n\n- for $-4<x<-1$, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=-1,$\n\n- for $-1<x<1$, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=0,$\n\n- for $1<x<4$, we have ${f}^{′}(x)=-1,$\n\n The derivative does not exist at $x=\\pm 1.$ So, we have  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & =0, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(1+h)-f(1)}{h} & =\\text{DNE}, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(3+h)-f(3)}{h} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only statements I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b613bf7-a641-547f-8487-d6af0da0f724" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Equivalent Definition of the Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33710c61-22d6-514d-a654-305c39257be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33710c61-22d6-514d-a654-305c39257be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506801} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd58159-33f5-5db7-909d-d7a72f3ebd4b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507249}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7fe715dd-222c-5303-8e5d-661af680f9ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-314062" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)$ and $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{f(x)-f\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}}{x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\,{f}^{′}\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078408 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b613bf7-a641-547f-8487-d6af0da0f724" :knowledge-point/title "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Equivalent Definition of the Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6a23679-6599-5401-aade-8321affdc088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6a23679-6599-5401-aade-8321affdc088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507184} :answer-field/id #uuid "101d9dd4-dd5a-5b40-bb06-cc4f4eee42ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "453b5d73-89c7-511f-b2be-1d3d4f762507" :question/math-academy-id "q-339039" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nL={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}.\n$$\n\nComparing this with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)-f(a)}{x-a},\n$$\n\nwe see that if we let $f(x)={x}^{2}$ and $a=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(5) & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{f(x)-f(5)}{x-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{5}^{2}}{x-5} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 5}\\frac{{x}^{2}-25}{x-5}=L.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis confirms that $L={f}^{′}(5).$\n\nComputing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2x,\\,{f}^{′}(5)=2\\cdot 5=10,\n$$\n\nand therefore, we conclude that $L=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186507463 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20df0d0d-a94e-55e6-8d19-25a566ab794d" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Limits Equivalent to a Derivative Using a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ebd984-f944-5735-935d-58b8e1d12c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506683} {:answer/id #uuid "3faecea9-9832-54b2-afe3-75ade1b40121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506827} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3d8d44-88a4-5931-b74f-49ee9adea60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506953} {:answer/id #uuid "c047a9a1-43b8-5862-adef-5be69f583fa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507100} {:answer/id #uuid "d19e8196-e4ed-521f-b41a-81344a83bfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507135}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d19e8196-e4ed-521f-b41a-81344a83bfe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507135} :answer-field/id #uuid "c755ac45-18f2-5688-96b0-fcf97a72209f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b3df8bd-2ae3-52a2-905f-ded2bd184071" :question/math-academy-id "q-48459" :question/problem "![](images/72/72e1d1f1ea1aa635441da5503a49ac4d33d5ab0df62d48aa699debc7816df1c2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the graph of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h}=1$\n\nII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h}=-0.5$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h}=1$" :question/worked-solution "Comparing these limits with the formula for the instantaneous rate of change\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwe see that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(-2), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(0), \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h} & ={f}^{′}(-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function consists of three line segments. Inspecting the graph, we see that\n\n- for $x<-1$, we have  ${f}^{′}(x)=1,$\n\n- for $-1<x<1$, we have  ${f}^{′}(x)=-1,$\n\n- for $x>1$, we have  ${f}^{′}(x)=1.$\n\nThe derivative does not exist at $x=-1$ and $x=1$ . So we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-2+h)-f(-2)}{h} & =1, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(h)-f(0)}{h} & =-1, \\\\ {lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(-3+h)-f(-3)}{h} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only I and III are true."}]}
